in mid-2020 as the coronavirus pandemic  was exploding around the world  this is what the situation looked like  right here in germany  the country was hailed the world's  shining star of coronavirus containment  germany germany by comparison germany  where hospitals are not yet overwhelmed  but the number of deaths in germany has  remained lower than many other countries  now things look very different than they  did last summer  while countries like the uk and israel  are going back to normalcy  [Applause]  germany has been under some form of  lockdown for over  six months on germany's intensive care  wards  this is what the third wave looks like  hospitals have been pleading with the  government to tighten restrictions  going on like this will destroy the  economy  will destroy the people and will cost  lives  so how did we get here how did germany  a country known for its efficiency love  of planning and rationality lose control  of its covet 19 response  problem one indecision germany was able  to flatten the first coronavirus curve  by  acting fast in march 2020 politicians  were quick to impose an  almost complete lockdown as the second  wave started picking up speed  critics say decision makers  underestimated the virus  much to the frustration of scientists  who had called for harsher measures  much earlier  it was november when politicians finally  took action  they imposed a partial lockdown  needless to say this partial lockdown  was  only partially successful  not only are these measures still in  place but they've been tightened  germany never went into a complete  lockdown  politicians were hesitant to fully shut  down workplaces such  as factories  on top of that new variants have made  the virus more aggressive  criticism of politicians indecision has  grown louder  moments  the inability of politicians to quickly  decide on uniform measures  it's partially down to how the country  is governed  germany is a federal republic decision  making is not centralized in the hands  of the chancellor in berlin  instead each of the country's 16 states  has its own government which can decide  on things like law enforcement or  education  this system is there for a reason  germany evolved from  hundreds of little royal states after  world war ii  federalism was enshrined in law to avoid  falling into a dictatorship  ever again but this system also means  when it comes to fighting the pandemic  that every government has the right to  decide on its own rules  in an attempt to find a unified approach  there have been regular discussions  between chancellor angela merkel  and the 16th state premiers  which unlike the spread of the  coronavirus can be  very very slow these meetings would  sometimes last  4 hours  they would go into details like should  schools open what about hairdressers  would a curfew help and the list goes on  in the end there would often  be no uniform measures to combat the  virus  people in the population are frustrated  nobody knows anymore what the  regulations are right now  i think it's chaos especially if you  look at the different vaccination  strategies  in early april a poll showed that four  out of five germans are unhappy with how  the federal  and state governments are handling the  pandemic another reason why germany has  had a hard time  getting out of this ongoing lockdown and  fighting the pandemic is  bureaucracy  [Music]  in a pandemic tracking cases is crucial  to break down infection chains  and slow down exponential growth in  germany it's the over 400 gesundheits  empta  or public health departments that are in  charge of tracking infections  their methods are sometimes very  outdated  they are still working with mostly with  pen papers telephone they try to do  contact tracing and it doesn't really  work  covet has exposed just how antiquated  germany's bureaucracy  actually is for example how much it  relies on this  this is a fax machine  [Music]  a technology that's nearing its 